WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:05.350
TLS 1.3 sets up an encrypted connection in a single round trip.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:09.338
We'll follow each message, see why one trip is enough, and trace

3
00:00:09.188 --> 00:00:11.725
every key back to one shared secret.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.650
Here is the full exchange.

5
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.275
The client speaks first, the server answers with a single flight of

6
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:21.625
messages, and the client closes with its own Finished.

7
00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:24.325
After that, application data flows.

8
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:26.600
Let's take the messages one at a time.

9
00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:30.975
The ClientHello opens with a random value and a list of cipher

10
00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:31.750
suites.

11
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.900
It names the groups it supports.

12
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.975
The key move is the key share.

13
00:00:35.962 --> 00:00:40.237
Instead of asking which group the server wants, the client guesses, usually

14
00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:45.350
X25519, and sends an ephemeral public key immediately.

15
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.000
Last come the host name and protocol.

16
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.725
The server picks a suite and answers with its own key share.

17
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.625
Now each side holds its private key and the other's public key,

18
00:00:56.612 --> 00:00:59.775
so both compute the same Diffie-Hellman secret.

19
00:00:59.788 --> 00:01:03.150
Everything the server sends after this point is encrypted.

20
00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:07.275
EncryptedExtensions carries leftover settings.

21
00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:10.050
The Certificate says who the server claims to be.

22
00:01:10.075 --> 00:01:14.887
CertificateVerify proves it: the server signs a hash of every message so

23
00:01:14.737 --> 00:01:18.050
far, which only the private key holder can do.

24
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:22.363
Finished is a MAC over the whole transcript, proving nobody tampered with

25
00:01:22.213 --> 00:01:23.925
any earlier message.

26
00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:29.413
The client validates the chain, checks the signature, and checks the server's

27
00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:30.300
Finished.

28
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.287
Then it sends its own Finished, and can send application data in

29
00:01:34.137 --> 00:01:35.800
the very same flight.

30
00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:39.350
The server can even send data straight after its own Finished.

31
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.975
In TLS 1.2, the first trip only agreed on algorithms, and the

32
00:01:44.825 --> 00:01:47.125
key exchange needed a second trip.

33
00:01:47.125 --> 00:01:51.750
TLS 1.3 merges them: the client sends its key share up front,

34
00:01:51.712 --> 00:01:53.125
betting on a group.

35
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.850
TLS 1.3 also trimmed the menu to a handful of strong groups,

36
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.200
so the bet almost always wins.

37
00:02:00.187 --> 00:02:03.975
That's how the first application data arrives after one round trip.

38
00:02:04.925 --> 00:02:08.738
If the server doesn't support the client's guessed group, it replies with

39
00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:11.700
a HelloRetryRequest naming the group it wants.

40
00:02:11.712 --> 00:02:15.775
The client sends a second ClientHello and the handshake costs two round

41
00:02:15.625 --> 00:02:16.550
trips.

42
00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:18.875
That's the worst case, and it's rare.

43
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.550
Every key descends from one schedule built on HKDF.

44
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.875
It starts with the early secret, made from a pre-shared key if

45
00:02:26.725 --> 00:02:29.250
resuming, or zeros otherwise.

46
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.050
Mixing in the Diffie-Hellman shared secret gives the handshake secret.

47
00:02:33.038 --> 00:02:36.913
From it come two traffic secrets, one per direction, that encrypt the

48
00:02:36.763 --> 00:02:39.350
server's flight and the client's Finished.

49
00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:43.225
One more step gives the master secret, and from that the application

50
00:02:43.075 --> 00:02:45.125
traffic secrets for real data.

51
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:50.175
Each derivation takes a parent secret, a label such as "s hs

52
00:02:50.025 --> 00:02:53.175
traffic", and a hash of the transcript so far.

53
00:02:53.188 --> 00:02:57.363
So the keys depend on every handshake message: if an attacker alters

54
00:02:57.213 --> 00:03:01.088
one byte of the ClientHello, both sides derive different keys and the

55
00:03:00.938 --> 00:03:02.700
Finished check fails.

56
00:03:02.675 --> 00:03:07.525
Because the Diffie-Hellman keys are ephemeral and thrown away, recorded traffic stays

57
00:03:07.375 --> 00:03:10.675
safe even if the server's certificate key leaks later.

58
00:03:10.737 --> 00:03:12.450
That's forward secrecy.

59
00:03:13.425 --> 00:03:18.075
To recap: the client guesses ahead, so one round trip suffices.

60
00:03:18.025 --> 00:03:20.650
The server encrypts almost everything.

61
00:03:20.663 --> 00:03:24.125
Signatures and MACs prove identity and integrity.

62
00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:27.738
And a single key schedule ties every key to the shared secret

63
00:03:27.588 --> 00:03:28.875
and the transcript.
